<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carl Ulrich</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1853">1853</a>–<a href="/years/1933">1933</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carl Ulrich was a German Social Democratic politician from Offenbach. After the revolution of 1918 he became the first President of the People's State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, governing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a> until 1928 at the head of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> coalition governments. He is regarded as the founder of democratic government in Hesse.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carl-ulrich.jpg" alt="Carl Ulrich" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carl_Ulrich_1870.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Carl Theodor Johann Ulrich was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Braunschweig</a> on 28 January 1853, the son of a shoemaker. He left school in 1867, trained as a mechanical engineer and in 1872 travelled through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. In 1874 he settled in Offenbach in the Grand Duchy of Hesse, worked in a machine tool factory and joined the metalworkers' union. Jailed for 30 days that December for labour organising, he was one of the youngest delegates at the founding congress of the Socialist Workers' Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1875, and the same year became editor of the Neue Offenbacher Tages-Zeitung.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1885 Ulrich was elected to both chambers of the Hessian parliament, becoming with Franz Jöst the first social democratic deputies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>. He sat in the lower house and its successors without a break until 1931, 46 years, the longest service of any legislator in German history. In 1886 he, August Bebel, Ignaz Auer and others were sentenced to nine months under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-socialist-laws">Anti-Socialist Laws</a>. By the end of the 1880s he led the party in Hesse, and he sat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> from 1890, apart from 1903 to 1907. A pragmatist who kept out of the party's quarrels over theory, he said in 1898: &quot;We don't float in the clouds, we live on the earth.&quot;</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>When the Hessian monarchy was abolished on 11 November 1918, Ulrich became head of the new republican state, leading a cabinet of the SPD, the German Democratic Party and the Zentrum. After the SPD won the elections of January 1919 he formed a second such cabinet, and he continued as State-President under the constitution of 20 March 1920, his government being returned in 1921, 1924 and 1927. From 1922 he also ran the education ministry and worked for universal primary schooling.</p><p>Known as the red grand duke, he received a letter of congratulation from the former Grand Duke Ernest Louis on his 75th birthday in 1928, and retired that year in favour of Bernhard Adelung. He died in Offenbach on 12 April 1933, and the Carl-Ulrich-Brücke over the Main bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Carl Ulrich” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Social Democrats led the state for most of the Weimar years under <b>Carl Ulrich</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/peoples-state-of-hesse">People's State of Hesse</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Brunswick, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offenbach-am-main">Offenbach am Main</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.27,10.52&amp;year=1905" aria-label="Brunswick on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4787 359 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Carl Ulrich"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5237 629h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5237 629h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.27,10.52&amp;year=1905">What was here in 1905? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
